.SH "CLEANING UP"
.B duscope clean
moves files and directories to the Trash, or with
.B \-\-permanent
deletes them.
It makes a plan first, and acts on it only with
.B \-\-yes
or a
.B yes
typed at its question: without either, nothing moves.
.SS "The plan"
A plan is made against one saved scan: the one
.B \-\-scan
names; else, when only handles name the items (see Handles), the scan
.B explain
read for them; else the newest that holds the first item.
The scan's root bounds what may go, and its sizes are the plan's.
With no saved scan,
.B clean
fails with
.B scan_not_found
(exit 3), and
.B duscope scan \-\-save
makes one.
For each item the plan gives its size on disk, what deleting it would free
now, the command that regenerates it, and what is done with it: a move to
the Trash, a deletion, or a refusal with its reason.
What an item frees is its bytes less those that a hard link or an APFS
clone outside the plan keeps: a file whose other links lie elsewhere frees
nothing, and two links both in the plan free their bytes once.
.PP
An item that has changed since the saved scan (its size, times or links,
or for a directory what it holds) is measured from the tree as it is now,
with the
.B changed_since_scan
warning; a directory measured so frees its bytes at most
.RB ( upper_bound ),
and one a walk could not read whole has no figure.
An item named twice, or inside another item, is a usage error (exit 2):
an overlap fails the whole list, before anything moves.
.PP
When stdin and stderr are a terminal and the output is for people,
.B clean
shows the plan and asks once, unless
.B \-\-no\-input
is given; only
.BR yes ,
typed in full, acts.
Otherwise, and after any other answer, it prints the plan and exits 1 with
.BR confirmation_required .
.B \-\-expect\-bytes
.I N
acts only when the plan frees within 1% of
.I N
bytes, and never when an item has no figure; otherwise nothing is touched
.RB ( expectation_mismatch ,
exit 1).
.SS Refusals
A refused item stops the whole run before anything moves, with
.B refused_path
(exit 1), unless
.B \-\-skip\-refused
is given, which acts on the other items.
The plan gives each refused item one of these reasons:
.TP
.B symlink
The item is a symbolic link.
.TP
.B firmlink
The item is a macOS firmlink, which leads from the system volume into the
Data volume.
.TP
.B mount_point
The item is a mount point; for the Trash, also a directory with a mount
point anywhere under it, or one whose mounts the system cannot tell, since
a move would take the mounted volume into the Trash with it.
On Linux a btrfs subvolume is a mount point to
.BR clean ,
and so is a file of an overlayfs mount where the system cannot tell
whether it is the root of a mount.
.TP
.B root
The item is the saved scan's root, or
.IR / .
.TP
.B outside_root
The item is not under the saved scan's root.
.TP
.B read_only_volume
The item's volume is mounted read\-only.
.TP
.B expectation_mismatch
Only under
.BR "duscope mcp" :
.B duscope_delete
refuses an item that does not free, within 1%, what the client said it
would.
.SS "The Trash and \-\-permanent"
By default each item is moved to the Trash, by renaming it, never by
copying it: on macOS into the Trash of the item's volume, through
NSFileManager; on Linux into the XDG Trash, the home Trash
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash ,
else
.IR ~/.local/share/Trash )
for an item on its filesystem and otherwise the volume's own
.RI ( .Trash/$uid
or
.IR .Trash\-$uid ),
with a
.I .trashinfo
file that says where the item was.
An item whose volume has no Trash duscope can use is left where it is
.RB ( trash_unavailable ):
.B clean
never falls back to deleting.
Moving to the Trash frees nothing until the Trash is emptied: the result
counts the bytes moved in
.B trashed_bytes
and leaves
.B freed_bytes
at 0.
.PP
.B \-\-permanent
deletes the items instead, and nothing brings them back.
Every directory on the way to an item is opened without following a
symbolic link, and a directory is emptied from the bottom up, never across
a mount point nor into a btrfs subvolume: there the item fails
.RB ( refused_path ),
what was deleted before stays deleted, and the hint names
.B umount
or
.BR "btrfs subvolume delete" .
.B freed_bytes
counts the bytes of each file whose last link went.
.PP
Just before each item goes,
.B clean
checks that its path still holds what the plan saw (its device and
inode): an item that has gone or been replaced fails alone
.RB ( path_changed ),
and the others still go.
A saved scan is never changed, so afterwards the saved scans still count
what went
.RB ( index_untouched ),
until
.B duscope rescan
measures the tree again.
.SS Handles
.B \-\-from
.I HANDLE
names an item by a handle from
.BR "explain \-\-json" :
the path as
.B explain
examined it, signed with
.I handle.key
(see FILES) and good for fifteen minutes.
Before anything moves,
.B clean
fails with
.B handle_invalid
for a handle this duscope did not sign,
.B handle_expired
for one past its fifteen minutes, and
.B path_changed
for one whose path no longer holds what
.B explain
examined, or holds it changed since the saved scan.
Handles of different saved scans are a usage error: a plan is made against
one.
